-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, activation, recovery, and lookup all belong to this node. Each kiosk keeps an immutable SQLite database for the active snapshot and retains that snapshot's signed source files so it can reconstruct the derived index locally. SQLite is an implementation detail behind the registry interface; no storage path, connection, or query type crosses into the certificates node.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Copy a candidate snapshot from approved USB media into a new staging generation on the kiosk. Verify its manifest and payload signature with the bundled public key before trusting record content. Reject unsupported schema versions, malformed records, invalid field values, and serial numbers that collide after lookup normalization.
+
+Build a new SQLite database in the staging generation. Store one row per record, keyed by the normalized serial number, and store the snapshot date, schema version, and signed-payload digest as database metadata. The record table contains only the equipment description, calibration date, and due date needed by the registry result. Build the unique primary-key index as records are loaded, then finish the database transaction and run SQLite's integrity check before activation.
+
+Each generation is immutable after preparation. Activate a generation by atomically replacing a small active-generation pointer only after its source files and SQLite database are durable. Lookups that began before the replacement continue against the prior generation; later lookups open the new generation. Keep the previously active generation until the new active generation has been opened successfully, so an interrupted import or failed first open cannot remove the last usable registry.
+
+Import errors identify the failed stage and a useful cause without activating the candidate. Staging generations left by interruption are not lookup candidates and can be removed on the next startup or import.
+
+## Startup and Recovery
+
+At startup, resolve the active-generation pointer and verify that the referenced database opens read-only, passes SQLite's quick integrity check, and has metadata matching the retained signed snapshot. If the database is absent, corrupt, or mismatched, rebuild it into a new staged database from that generation's already verified source files and atomically replace the damaged derived database. Do not activate source files whose signature or schema cannot be validated during recovery.
+
+If recovery cannot produce a valid database, try the retained previous generation. If neither generation is usable, expose the registry as unavailable rather than returning `NotFound`, because absence of a usable snapshot is not evidence that a serial number is absent.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters once to produce the SQLite primary key. Serial-number punctuation and internal whitespace remain significant. Perform an exact primary-key query through a read-only SQLite connection; full-text and partial matching are outside the registry interface.
+
+Return `Found(record, snapshotDate)` for one matching row and `NotFound(snapshotDate)` when the active database contains no matching key. Read the record and snapshot metadata from the same immutable generation so activation cannot combine a record from one snapshot with another snapshot's date.
+
+## Resource Bounds
+
+Stream newline-delimited JSON into SQLite rather than retaining the snapshot in memory. The database primary-key index is the only lookup index required for serial-number lookup. Import may take longer than a lookup, but it runs entirely in staging and does not block reads from the active generation. These choices keep lookup work bounded to an indexed exact query on the kiosk and keep import memory use independent of the 150 MB snapshot size.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Per-kiosk embedded SQLite index | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | SQLite keeps every lookup local and predictable, requires no service or credentials, and fits the fixed exact-match interface. Each kiosk accepts the cost of validating and rebuilding its own derived index when a signed snapshot arrives. |
+| Snapshot activation | Immutable generation plus atomic active pointer | Replace one database in place; update one long-lived database transactionally | Generation switching avoids exposing partial imports and permits readers and recovery to retain the last usable snapshot without coupling activation to open-database replacement behavior. |
+| Index key | Unique normalized serial number | Raw serial number; separate non-unique normalized column | Enforcing uniqueness on the exact lookup key prevents two source records from becoming ambiguous after case and surrounding-whitespace normalization. |
+
